      "Listings give capacities from 450 lb to 1,250 lb; GMWD’s own listing says 450 lb total and 200 lb per arm, so plan on the lowest",
      "A light 2 x 2 in, 14-gauge frame, nothing like the commercial machines discussed here",
      "Near-identical machines are sold under other brand names; buy the GMWD listing if you want GMWD’s support",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a beginner or a small room that wants to try a converging press before spending four figures. Respect the lowest capacity figure.",
    description: "This is GMWD’s entry chest press, a folding bench with two independent arms that GMWD’s Amazon listing says converge. GMWD lists 11 leverage positions for the arms, three bench positions (upright, incline and flat) plus a leg attachment, precision bearings and detachable sleeves for 1 in and 2 in plates, and says it folds with a locking pin. Its listing gives 450 lb total, 200 lb per arm and 200 lb per weight holder, while other listings and roundups of what appears to be the same machine give 1,050, 1,200 or 1,250 lb, so we treat 450 lb as the working limit and leave capacity off the specification line. GMWD gives the footprint as 64.8 x 55.8 x 26.8 in; another retailer gives the same 64.8 in length with the other two figures arranged differently, so confirm it with GMWD. The frame is 2 x 2 in, 14-gauge steel. We saw it at $189.99 on Amazon on 9 November 2026. It takes the place the Precor Discovery Series chest press held on this page: Precor’s plate-loaded line is sold by quote through Precor and dealers, not on Amazon.",
